#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""homelab-cards - gives every new container that has a web page its own card on the Homepage dashboard.

  sudo homelab-cards                  look now (same as: sync): add cards for new services, keep the cards it made correct
  sudo homelab-cards list             the cards it manages, the services it is waiting for, and the ones it ignores
  sudo homelab-cards check            look only: is every card it manages in order? (used by homelab-selftest)
  sudo homelab-cards move CARD SECTION   put a card in another section (CARD is the container name, card id or title)
  sudo homelab-cards ignore NAME      never make a card for this container (and remove the one it made)
  sudo homelab-cards unignore NAME    allow it again
  sudo homelab-cards sync --dry-run   show what would change and change nothing
  homelab-cards version | help

The same command also runs by itself every 5 minutes (root's crontab: homelab-cards auto) and says nothing unless it changes something.

What it does for a container that is not one of the lab's own apps, has a port published on this machine and answers on it like a web page:
  - guesses the section from the image and container name: Network & DNS, Monitoring & Management, Files & Photos or Media & Entertainment
    (when it cannot tell, the card goes into Monitoring & Management and the login screen asks you to move it: sudo homelab-cards move NAME SECTION)
  - adds the card to ~/homepage-config/services.yaml (name, link, up/down dot, icon, short text) at the end of that section
  - adds its colour to ~/homepage-config/custom.css, in the same lists as the other cards
  - gives it a Tailscale HTTPS address (the next free port from 1009), so the card's link works from anywhere
  - keeps the card's addresses right when the IP address or the Tailscale name changes
  - removes the card (and its Tailscale address and colour) when the container is gone for good (two looks in a row, 5 minutes apart)
It never touches cards it did not make (your own hand-written ones, like Metube and Downtify before this command existed).
The old services.yaml and custom.css are saved once as services.yaml.before-cards and custom.css.before-cards.
Image updates need nothing here: sudo homelab-update already looks after every running container.
"""
import fcntl
